(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-heart-failure-quiz"]={"title":"Heart Failure Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 4","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Besides signs and symptoms from a structural or functional cardiac problem, what else does clinical heart failure require?","choices":["Abnormal pressures measured by right heart catheterization","Elevated natriuretic peptide or objective cardiogenic congestion","An ejection fraction measured at 35% or less on imaging","A documented prior myocardial infarction on the electrocardiogram"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Clinical heart failure needs the signs and symptoms plus one of two supports: an elevated B-type natriuretic peptide, or objective evidence of cardiogenic pulmonary or systemic congestion. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which is one of the four basic mechanisms of heart failure?","choices":["Reduced blood volume","Excessive afterload","Increased contractility","Increased filling"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The four are increased blood volume (excess preload), increased resistance (excess afterload), decreased contractility and decreased filling. Each leads through acute damage or chronic stress to remodeling. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"In suspected right-sided heart failure, which test gives the definitive diagnosis?","choices":["Natriuretic peptide level","Electrocardiogram","Echocardiogram","Right heart catheterization"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Clinical heart failure with jugular venous distension raises suspicion of right-sided disease, and the echocardiogram gives the definitive diagnosis by ejection fraction. Right heart catheterization then grades hemodynamic severity. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is N-terminal pro-B-type natriuretic peptide?","choices":["An inactive precursor fragment","The biologically active hormone","The enzyme that degrades the hormone","A marker of dead heart muscle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is the inactive precursor fragment released alongside the active hormone. It is more stable, peaks around 24 hours, and is detected earlier and for longer than B-type natriuretic peptide. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What does a clinical diagnosis of congestive heart failure require?","choices":["A fourth heart sound heard on auscultation","Congestion, meaning pulmonary or peripheral edema","An ejection fraction within the reduced range","Symptoms lasting longer than one full year"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Clinical heart failure means the patient is symptomatic, and calling it congestive requires congestion, which is edema in the lungs, the periphery or both. Fatigue and exertional dyspnea without edema do not meet it. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which drug class is recommended to prevent heart failure in patients with diabetes who have no structural heart disease?","choices":["Beta blocker started at diagnosis","Angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor","Hydralazine with isosorbide dinitrate","Sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Prevention in stage A, risk factors with a normal heart, carries one strong drug recommendation: a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor for patients with diabetes, ahead of any beta blocker, which is a stage B therapy. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"In the diastolic heart failure chain, how does edema progress?","choices":["Peripheral only, never pulmonary","Peripheral first, then pulmonary","Pulmonary first, then peripheral","Pulmonary only, never peripheral"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The diastolic chain ends in peripheral edema and then pulmonary edema, while the systolic chain goes straight to pulmonary edema. The order is one of the discriminators between them. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which referral accompanies optimized therapy after acute heart failure leaves the ejection fraction below 35%?","choices":["Cardiac rehabilitation","Hospice enrollment","Transplant evaluation","Valve surgery review"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac rehabilitation runs alongside optimized guideline-directed therapy while the patient wears a defibrillator vest, before the echocardiogram is repeated to decide on an implantable device. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which ejection fraction range defines heart failure with mildly reduced ejection fraction?","choices":["51% to 59%","20% to 35%","41% to 49%","30% to 39%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mildly reduced ejection fraction is 41 to 49%. It sits between reduced at 40% or less and preserved at 50% or more, and its treatment resembles that of preserved ejection fraction. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which bedside hemodynamic profile describes congestion with adequate perfusion?","choices":["Warm and wet","Warm and dry","Cold and dry","Cold and wet"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The four profiles cross congestion with perfusion: warm and dry is normal, warm and wet is congestion, cold and dry is hypoperfusion, and cold and wet is hypoperfusion with congestion. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which therapy carries the strongest recommendation in mildly reduced ejection fraction?","choices":["Neprilysin inhibitor combination","Diuretics as needed","Evidence-based beta blocker","Mineralocorticoid antagonist"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In mildly reduced ejection fraction diuretics as needed are the one strong recommendation; the sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor is moderate, and renin-angiotensin blockade, the antagonist and the beta blocker are weak. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which description defines heart failure?","choices":["Persistently low systolic blood pressure from any cause","Blockage of coronary flow causing death of heart muscle","Output below demand, or met only at high filling pressures","Any ejection fraction that falls below the normal range"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The heart cannot pump blood at a rate that meets the body's metabolic needs at rest and during effort, or manages it only because filling pressures are abnormally high. That second half is why a normal output does not exclude the syndrome. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which blood test anchors the laboratory workup of suspected heart failure?","choices":["B-type natriuretic peptide","Cardiac troponin level","Complete blood count","Serum potassium alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The natriuretic peptide is the key laboratory test: it supports the clinical diagnosis, rules acute heart failure out below 100, and carries prognostic weight. The blood count, metabolic panel and troponin play supporting roles. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Diastolic heart failure traces back to which cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Dilated cardiomyopathy","Ischemic cardiomyopathy","Toxin-related cardiomyopathy","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Increased muscle contracts strongly but relaxes poorly, so filling suffers. The chain runs diastolic failure, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, hypertension, a fourth heart sound, then peripheral and pulmonary edema. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"An ejection fraction that rises from 30% to 46% on treatment is classified as which group?","choices":["Mildly reduced ejection fraction","Preserved ejection fraction","Reduced ejection fraction","Improved ejection fraction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A previous value in the reduced range that has recovered above 40% is improved ejection fraction, even though the new number alone would fall in the mildly reduced band. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which toxins cause heart failure?","choices":["Caffeine and nicotine gum","Lead and arsenic salts","Aspirin and acetaminophen","Alcohol and chemotherapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Toxins such as alcohol and chemotherapy sit among the non-ischemic causes of heart failure, alongside hypertension, valvular disease and autoimmune or metabolic disease. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which workup is used for suspected high-output heart failure?","choices":["Troponin and a stress test only","Chest radiograph and spirometry","Echo, blood count, metabolic panel, peptide","Right heart catheterization by itself"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The high-output workup is an echocardiogram, complete blood count, complete metabolic panel and B-type natriuretic peptide. The blood count matters because anemia is one of its causes. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Among patients older than 65 with heart failure, what is the one-year mortality after a hospitalization?","choices":["About 35%","About 5%","About 15%","About 70%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. One-year mortality after a heart failure hospitalization is about 35% in this older group, which is why heart failure is described as a syndrome with reduced longevity. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which drugs are sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitors used in heart failure?","choices":["Dapagliflozin and empagliflozin","Spironolactone and eplerenone","Losartan and irbesartan","Furosemide and bumetanide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Dapagliflozin and empagliflozin are the sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitors, recommended from stage A in diabetes through every symptomatic ejection fraction group. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What should a patient starting a loop diuretic expect to have monitored?","choices":["Natriuretic peptide weekly","Potassium and kidney function","Liver enzymes and bilirubin","Thyroid-stimulating hormone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics waste potassium, so potassium and renal function are followed on the metabolic panel. The same monitoring applies in the other direction for the potassium-sparing antagonists. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What guidance applies to verapamil and diltiazem in heart failure?","choices":["Prefer them to beta blockers","Give them in any class","Avoid them","Use them first-line"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Verapamil and diltiazem are calcium channel blockers, and calcium channel blockers are avoided in heart failure. Rate and pressure control come from the recommended classes instead. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which patients with reduced ejection fraction receive hydralazine with isosorbide dinitrate?","choices":["African American patients in functional class III or IV","Every patient in functional class I or II at diagnosis","Patients with preserved ejection fraction","At-risk patients who also have diabetes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hydralazine with nitrates carries a strong recommendation in reduced ejection fraction for African American patients in New York Heart Association class III or IV, added to the core drug classes. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which renal complication of heart failure reflects inadequate perfusion?","choices":["Nephrotic syndrome with heavy proteinuria","Kidney stones from urinary stasis","Polyuria from excess filtration","Acute kidney injury with low urine output"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Renal dysfunction in heart failure presents as acute kidney injury with decreased urine output, one of the signs of inadequate tissue perfusion. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which N-terminal pro-B-type natriuretic peptide level suggests heart failure in a patient younger than 75?","choices":["Above 900","Above 2,000","Above 125","Above 450"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Younger than 75, a level above 125 makes heart failure likely; older than 75 the threshold rises to 450. Age changes how the same number is read. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What separates New York Heart Association class II from class III?","choices":["Class III needs only slight exertion","Class III has symptoms at rest","Class II has no symptoms at all","Class III requires structural disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both are free of symptoms at rest. Class II has symptoms with exertion, while class III has them with slight exertion, so the amount of effort that provokes symptoms separates them. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which predischarge B-type natriuretic peptide is linked to a lower risk of 30-day readmission?","choices":["Below 1,500","Below 430","Above 430","Above 900"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A predischarge level below 430 carries a lower risk of readmission within 30 days, which makes the level a guide to readiness for discharge. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Structural heart disease with symptoms that have since resolved on treatment belongs to which stage?","choices":["Stage D","Stage C","Stage B","Stage A"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stage C is symptomatic structural heart disease, past or current. Once symptoms have occurred the patient stays in stage C when they resolve; the stages do not move backward. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which metabolic condition is a risk factor for heart failure?","choices":["Iron overload from diet alone","Low blood cholesterol levels","Obesity or metabolic syndrome","Low body mass with good fitness"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Obesity and metabolic syndrome are risk factors, along with diabetes, hypertension and coronary artery disease. They are also targets a patient can change. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which murmur accompanies mitral stenosis as a cause of right-sided heart failure?","choices":["Holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border","Systolic murmur at the base radiating to the neck","Continuous murmur beneath the left clavicle","Diastolic rumble at the apex with an opening snap"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mitral stenosis gives a rumbling diastolic murmur at the apex with an opening snap. It is one of the three causes of right-sided failure, alongside chronic left-sided failure and pulmonary arterial hypertension. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What are the goals of treatment in chronic heart failure?","choices":["Cure of the cardiomyopathy in every case","A normal ejection fraction in every patient","Stopping all drugs once symptoms settle","Fewer symptoms, longer life, slower disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The three goals are to decrease symptoms, increase survival and limit disease progression. That is why several drug classes are combined even when a patient feels well. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 40"}]};
